# Comments and Formatting

## Comments

- **Code should be self-explanatory**. If you need a comment, the code is not clear enough
- **Never comment what the code does**. The code already expresses that
- **Only comment WHY, never WHAT**. Explain non-obvious decisions or trade-offs
- **Avoid JavaDoc in internal code** unless it adds real value (public APIs or libraries are the exception)
- **Delete commented code**. Version control already keeps history

```java
// ⚠️ WORSE - Commenting what
// Increment counter by 1
counter++;

// ⚠️ WORSE - Commented code
// double oldValue = calculate();
double newValue = calculate();

// ✅ BETTER - Self-explanatory code
double invoiceTotal = calculateInvoiceTotal(lineItems);
```

```java
// ⚠️ WORSE - Redundant JavaDoc
/**
 * Calculates the total price of an order
 * @param order The order
 * @return total price
 */
public double calc(Order order) {
    return order.getItems()
            .stream()
            .mapToDouble(Item::getPrice)
            .sum();
}

// ✅ BETTER - Clear method name and signature
public double calculateTotal(Order order) {
    return order.getItems()
            .stream()
            .mapToDouble(Item::getPrice)
            .sum();
}
```

## Formatting

- Let the formatter handle it (IntelliJ / Eclipse formatter, Checkstyle, Spotless)
- Use consistent indentation (spaces, not tabs) — typically 4 spaces in Java
- One statement per line
- **Avoid unnecessary blank lines inside methods**. Use spacing only to separate logical blocks if truly needed
- Always use braces {} for conditionals and loops (even single-line)

```java
// ❌ BAD
if (isValid) doSomething();

// ✅ GOOD
if (isValid) {
    doSomething();
}
```